coder › Done, on branch `fix/csv-formula-injection`, commit `1dd6746`.

**What changed — `src/csv.ts`:**

```ts
export type Cell = string | number

/** Spreadsheets treat a field starting with one of these as a formula. */
const FORMULA_START = /^[=+\-@\t\r]/

function field(cell: Cell): string {
  if (typeof cell === 'number') return String(cell)
  const quoted = `"${cell.replace(/"/g, '""')}"`
  if (FORMULA_START.test(cell)) return `'${quoted}`
  return /[",\n]/.test(cell) ? quoted : cell
}
```

- A string field starting with `=`, `+`, `-`, `@`, tab or CR is emitted as `'"<field>"` — quote prefix, whole field quoted, embedded double quotes doubled — so the spreadsheet shows text.
- A number field (negative included) is written plainly and never quoted; the `'` prefix also can't leak into one, since `field` branches on `typeof cell === 'number'` rather than on the stringified value.
- The existing comma / double quote / newline quoting is unchanged for safe fields.

**Tests — `src/csv.test.ts`** (was 1 test, now 7): kept your comma test; added double-quote doubling, newline quoting, plain negative numbers, one test looping over all six formula-leading strings (asserting `'` prefix plus quoting), a safe-string case (`a=b` stays bare), and `=a,b` to pin the prefix-plus-quoting combination.

**Checks:** `bun test` → `7 pass, 0 fail, 12 expect() calls`. Nothing else in the repo (no lint/typecheck config; the package's only script is `test`).

One thing worth flagging: the original quoting rule only handles `\n`, not `\r`, so a field containing a lone carriage return still goes out unquoted — I left that as-is since you scoped the change to leading characters, but it's a one-character fix (`/[",\n\r]/`) if you want it.

